## Previously raised findings (review-round registry)

These defects were raised on earlier review rounds of this PR. For each finding below: verify fixed (cite the fixing hunk), withdraw (reason required), or mark re-introduced. New claims need new evidence — a claim already on this list is answered, not re-raised.

| id | status | severity | file | claim |
|----|--------|----------|------|-------|
| F1 | open | IMPORTANT | flutter_app/lib/l10n/l10n_ext.dart | ⚠️ important the new handoff override doesn t reach context free locale surfaces resolveapplocale gained a localeoverride input but only materialapp localeresolutioncallback receives it main dart 295 322 the other call site of the app s one locale decision — agent service assistant dart 152 158 the 429 rate limit bubble feeding formatratelimitmessage localecode … — still resolves from the bare platformdispatcher locale on the exact deployment this pr targets branded web handoff lang ru or stored ain lang ru on an en locale device the onboarding chat ui resolves russian while rate limit failure copy stays english — exactly the surface drift this file s own contract rules out dart so an in app override or a changed supported set cannot drift between surfaces flutter app lib l10n l10n ext dart 16 19 issue 867 thread suggested fix compute the override once at boot and route it through the same decision — e g a applocaleoverride getter or a resolveapplocale wrapper that agent service assistant dart also consults — and extend app locale test dart to pin the handoff case for context free surfaces |

### How to answer

Add a `findingReview` block to `outputs/pr_review.json` (alongside `inlineComments`):

```json
{"findingReview": {
  "dispositions": [
    {"n": 1, "status": "fixed", "hunk": "<file:lines \u2014 the fixing hunk>"},
    {"n": 2, "status": "withdrawn", "reason": "<why this is not a defect>"},
    {"n": 3, "status": "re-introduced"}
  ],
  "confirmations": [
    {"proposedFor": 1, "same": true}
  ]
}}
```

Unconfirmed candidates become new identities (the merge bias is confirm-only); a genuinely new finding on a resolved item is expected to be raised again with new evidence.

